<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William Goffe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1605–c. 1679</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William Goffe was an English Parliamentarian officer and one of the judges who signed the death warrant of Charles I in 1649, later a major-general under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Oliver Cromwell</a>. At the Restoration in 1660 he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a> with his father-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-whalley">Edward Whalley</a>. They hid in the New Haven Colony, including a cave on West Rock, and later at Hadley, Massachusetts, where Goffe died in hiding.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-goffe.jpg" alt="William Goffe" width="320" height="451" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_Goffe_(page_79_crop).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Goffe was the fourth of five sons of the Reverend Stephen Goffe, rector of Bramber in Sussex, who lost that living in 1607 for helping to organise a Puritan petition to James I. His brothers Stephen and John became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a> priests, and Stephen later acted as a Royalist agent and converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>. In 1634 William was apprenticed to William Vaughan, a Presbyterian London drysalter of the Company of Grocers. Around 1650 he married Frances Whalley, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-whalley">Edward Whalley</a>, a cousin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Oliver Cromwell</a>; they had three daughters.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Briefly imprisoned in early 1642 for a petition demanding that control of the London Trained Bands pass to Parliament, Goffe was a captain by July 1643 and fought at the First Battle of Newbury. He joined the New Model Army in 1645 and served at Naseby, Langport and Torrington. A believer that the Second Coming was near, known to contemporaries as Praying William, he argued at the Putney Debates of 1647 that Charles I should be tried and that those still negotiating with him were thwarting God's will, which drew a rebuke from Cromwell.</p><p>After fighting at Preston in 1648 he was one of the fifty-nine judges who approved the king's execution. He commanded Cromwell's own infantry regiment at Dunbar in 1650, became its colonel and fought at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/worcester">Worcester</a> a year later. A loyal supporter of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a>, he sat for Great Yarmouth in 1654, helped suppress the Penruddock uprising in 1655 and, as major-general, administered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berkshire">Berkshire</a>, Sussex and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hampshire">Hampshire</a>. He was named to Cromwell's Other House, but his influence ended when Richard Cromwell resigned as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-protector">Lord Protector</a> in 1659.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Goffe and Whalley sailed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> on 13 May 1660, a day before warrants were issued for their arrest, and at first lived openly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> with Daniel Gookin. Once it was confirmed that they had been excluded from the Indemnity and Oblivion Act, they moved in March 1661 to New Haven, where the minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-davenport">John Davenport</a> housed them, and from 1664 they were sheltered at Hadley by John Russell. Goffe's letters to his wife show that he kept his convictions and ran a small trading business.</p><p>His last letter is dated April 1679; he is thought to lie in an unmarked grave beside Whalley. In the 19th century he was suggested as the Angel of Hadley, said to have helped repel an attack on the town in 1675, though both the incident and his part in it are disputed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “William Goffe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1661 the colony sheltered the regicides Edward Whalley and <b>William Goffe</b> from royal agents.</blockquote><a href="/state/new-haven-colony">New Haven Colony</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53,-1&amp;year=1653" aria-label="England on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of William Goffe"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4949 5597h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4949 5597h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53,-1&amp;year=1653">What was here in 1653? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
